.TH CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT \- initializer for chttpsvr_config_t with reasonable defaults
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_config_t cfg = CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Expands to a compound literal that initializes a chttpsvr_config_t
with these defaults:

  host                  = NULL   (dual-stack, every interface;
                                "0.0.0.0" = every IPv4 interface only)
  port                  = 8080
  max_body_size         = 4 MiB  (0 = no limit)
  read_timeout_us       = 60000000   (60 s; 0 = no idle timeout)
  idle_timeout_us       = 60000000   (60 s; 0 = use read_timeout_us)
  stream_read_timeout_us = 30000000   (30 s; 0 = wait forever)
  max_body_read_duration_us = 300000000   (5 min; 0 = no cap on total
                                           read time)
  response_write_timeout_us = 0   (0 = use the value of
                                   stream_read_timeout_us)
  max_response_write_duration_us = 300000000   (5 min; 0 = no cap)
  max_header_read_duration_us = 30000000   (30 s; 0 = no cap)
  max_header_bytes     = 0   (0 = library default, 64 KiB)
  max_connections       = 16384  (0 = unlimited)
  enable_keepalive      = false
  enable_reuseport      = false
  ipv6_only             = false
  tls                   = NULL
  worker_thread_count   = 0   (0 = CPU core count)
  worker_queue_capacity = 0   (0 = 1024 * worker_thread_count)
  min_transfer_rate_bps = 240   (0 = 240; CHTTPSVR_NO_RATE_FLOOR = off)
  min_transfer_rate_grace_us = 5000000   (5 s; 0 = 5 s)
  max_partial_body_memory = 256 MiB   (0 = 256 MiB;
                                       CHTTPSVR_NO_MEMORY_CAP = no limit)
  body_memory_wait_timeout_us = 30000000   (30 s; 0 = 30 s;
                                            CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE = none)
  streaming_thread_count = 0   (0 = worker_thread_count;
                                CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF = share the
                                worker pool)
  streaming_queue_capacity = 0   (0 = 4 * streaming_thread_count;
                                  CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED = no limit)
  streaming_queue_timeout_us = 5000000   (5 s; 0 = 5 s;
                                          CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE = none)

Every timeout and duration field is a uint64_t count of microseconds.
The server keeps each one in whole milliseconds: it rounds a value up
to the next millisecond, so a value above 0 never reads as 0.  A value
longer than the server can keep saturates to the longest finite limit
that it keeps for that field, which is never shorter than 24 days, and
never turns into 0 or into "no limit".  CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE, in the
two fields that accept it, is the one value that means no limit.

Every timeout and every resource limit above is finite.  A server that
starts from these defaults is therefore already bounded against two
kinds of peer.  The first opens a connection and then stalls.  The
second sends a request body one byte at a time, or reads a response
one byte at a time.  You can change each limit on its own.  For the
fields up to ipv6_only, a value of 0 keeps its documented meaning for a
caller that asks for it explicitly.  The fields from
min_transfer_rate_bps to streaming_queue_timeout_us read 0 as "use the
default", so that a configuration built with {0}, or with designated
initializers that leave them out, keeps these defenses.  Each of them
turns off only through its own named constant.

host follows the rules of the listener of the Go net package.  NULL
or "" listens on every interface with one dual-stack socket on [::],
which also takes IPv4 clients through v4-mapped addresses, or on
0.0.0.0 on a host without IPv6.  A literal address listens on exactly
that address.  A host name listens on its first IPv4 address when it
has one, and on its first IPv6 address otherwise, so "localhost"
listens on 127.0.0.1.

host may also be a "unix://path/to/socket" string.  That binds a Unix
domain socket at that path instead of a TCP listener, and the server
then ignores port.  A socket file at that path that no process listens
on any more is replaced.  The start fails, and leaves the file alone,
when the path names anything that is not a socket, or a socket that a
live server listens on.

A sweep thread for the idle timeout applies read_timeout_us and
idle_timeout_us.  That thread belongs to this module, there is one for
the whole process, and every chttpsvr instance shares it.  No OS timer
for each connection applies these two fields.  They bound how long a
connection can sit idle between requests, that is, while it waits for
the headers of the next pipelined or keep-alive request.  They do not
bound how long a request can take while a worker thread handles it.
See max_body_read_duration_us for that case.

stream_read_timeout_us bounds the gap between one batch of body bytes
and the *next* while the server reads a request body.  It applies to a
buffered body and to a streaming body.  It is separate from
read_timeout_us and idle_timeout_us.  Those two reset on any
connection activity, so they do not protect you against a client that
sends its bytes just fast enough to always beat them.  After
stream_read_timeout_us expires, chttpsvr_req_read returns -1 and
chttpsvr_req_stream_error gives ccol_timed_out.  A buffered route
answers 408 automatically.

Note: stream_read_timeout_us itself also resets on every new byte.  A
client that sends a byte or two just before each gap expires therefore
defeats it, in the same way as it defeats read_timeout_us and
idle_timeout_us.  max_body_read_duration_us closes that loophole.  It
separately caps the *total* wall-clock time that the server spends to
read the body of one request, whatever number of single gaps it took
to get there.  Its default is 300000000 (5 minutes).  That default is
large enough for an upload of max_body_size bytes over a link so slow
that the transfer itself is the bottleneck.  After it expires, it
reports the same ccol_timed_out result as an expiry of
stream_read_timeout_us.

A value of 0 in stream_read_timeout_us, max_body_read_duration_us,
response_write_timeout_us or max_response_write_duration_us means
"wait forever".  Such a value does not make an unresponsive connection
a permanent problem.  A shutdown of the server still completes in
bounded time, even when a streaming handler really is blocked while it
reads such a connection.  A shutdown is a call to chttpsvr_destroy or
a call to chttpsvr_engine_stop; a restart with chttpsvr_stop and
chttpsvr_start waits for nothing and cuts nothing off.  The server
gets this bound by a
forced close of that connection, after its own bounded and graceful
wait for the in-flight requests ends.  This only happens as a part of
that shutdown sequence.  It never affects a request that still makes
progress.

response_write_timeout_us bounds the gap between two writes that make
progress, while the server sends one response to a client that reads
slowly.  It is the write-side equivalent of stream_read_timeout_us.
Progress is the client taking bytes of the response: the send queue of
the socket shrinking, which the sweep checks about once a second,
counts as much as a write that the kernel accepts.  A client that
reads a large response steadily therefore never meets this limit,
however large a send buffer the kernel gives the socket.  A client
that stops reading is cut between one and two sweep intervals after
the limit passes.  For a TCP client, min_transfer_rate_bps judges the
bytes that reached the client, not the bytes that the kernel
accepted.

max_response_write_duration_us is the write-side equivalent of
max_body_read_duration_us.  response_write_timeout_us bounds only each
single call equivalent to write(2).  A client that reads a byte or two
just before the timeout of each such call expires can therefore keep a
response going for as long as it wants.  max_response_write_duration_us
separately caps the *total* wall-clock time that the server spends to
send one response, whatever number of single calls it took to get
there.  Its default is 300000000 (5 minutes), for the same reasons as the
default of max_body_read_duration_us.  After it expires, the send of
the response fails and the server closes the connection.  The client
sees a truncated response or a reset.

A small internal ceiling of 2 seconds always bounds two more writes,
whatever value you set here, and this includes a value of 0.  The
first write is a courtesy rejection response (400, 404, 405, 408, 413,
500, 501, 503 or 505) that the server generates itself and not a
handler.  The second is the interim "100 Continue" line for "Expect:
100-continue".  Both always have a fixed, small shape, and neither has
a legitimate reason to need longer.  Neither holds a thread while it
waits for the peer: a write that meets a full socket parks the
connection, and the ceiling bounds how long it stays parked.  The
interim line of a streaming route is the exception: chttpsvr_req_read
writes it on the thread of the handler, which waits for the client
there as it waits for the body, and the ceiling bounds that wait.  A
value here below that ceiling still applies in full.

max_header_read_duration_us is the header-phase equivalent of
max_body_read_duration_us.  read_timeout_us and idle_timeout_us
measure the gap between one piece of activity and the next, and every
byte that arrives resets them.  A peer that keeps sending its request
headers, or its TLS handshake, one byte at a time, forever, and never
completes them therefore defeats both of them.  It never comes near
max_header_bytes either, because it never finishes the header block at
all.  max_header_read_duration_us separately caps the *total*
wall-clock time that one connection can spend in the reactor-owned
phase of one request.  That phase is the TLS handshake, where there is
one, plus the read of the whole header block of that request.  After
it expires, the server closes the connection and sends no response,
because the header block was never complete enough to answer.

The budget is for each request, and not for each connection.  It
starts at the first TLS handshake step for a connection that has one,
and at the first byte of the header block of the request for one that
does not.  The next request of a keep-alive connection starts a fresh
budget when its own first byte arrives.  Time that the connection
spends idle between two requests is therefore never charged to either
one.  The shared idle-timeout sweep thread notices the expiry, in the
same way as it does for read_timeout_us and idle_timeout_us.  That
sweep runs once a second and closes every connection that expired,
however many there are.  A connection that is past its deadline
therefore lives up to about a second longer than that deadline.

The default of 30 s is generous next to any legitimate client.  Such a
client sends its header block immediately after it connects.  Even a
full TLS handshake plus several kilobytes of headers over a lossy link
with high latency takes only seconds.  Raise it for a deployment whose
clients really are slower than that.  Set it to 0 to turn the limit
off when something else in front of this server already bounds the
same phase.

max_header_bytes bounds the combined size of the header block of one
request.  That block is the request line plus every header line.  The
server answers a request whose headers are above this with 400 Bad
Request and then closes the connection.  The request parser of this
module reports every other malformed request before the routing in the
same way.  The answer is best effort: a peer that is still streaming
its oversized header block when the server closes can meet a reset
before it reads anything.  Two fixed limits apply beside it, whatever
its value, with the same 400: the request line and each header line
must end within 8 KiB, CRLF included, and a request holds at most 100
header lines.  See chttpsvr_start(3) for the other requests that the
server refuses before the routing.

max_connections bounds how many connections this listener can have
open at the same time.  A finite value bounds how many file
descriptors one listener can hold.  A peer that opens connections
faster than the idle sweep frees them therefore cannot use up the
descriptor limit of the process, and cannot take down every other
listener with it.  Raise it for a deployment that really serves more
connections at the same time than the default.  Raise RLIMIT_NOFILE to
match.  At capacity, new connections stay pending in the listen
backlog of the kernel.  The server does not accept them and then
reject them.  It does not call accept(2) again until a connection
closes and frees a slot.  The listener stops accepting completely
while it is at capacity, and resumes automatically after a slot frees.
The worst case is about one second between the free of a slot and the
moment that the listener notices it.

enable_keepalive sets SO_KEEPALIVE on every connection that the server
accepts.  enable_reuseport sets SO_REUSEPORT on the socket that
listens.  That lets more than one chttpsvr instance, for example one
for each worker process, bind the same host and port, so that the
kernel load-balances the accepts.  ipv6_only sets IPV6_V6ONLY on an
AF_INET6 listener, so that the listener does not also accept
IPv4-mapped connections, and it makes a host name listen on its IPv6
address when it has one.  With ipv6_only off, a NULL or "" host is
always dual-stack, and an IPv6 address or name keeps the default of
the system.  The server applies enable_keepalive and enable_reuseport
as well as it can: a setsockopt failure does not make chttpsvr_start
fail.  All three have no effect on a Unix domain socket listener.

No worker thread waits out any of these read and write limits for a
buffered route.  A body that stops arriving, or a response that meets a
full socket, parks its connection with no thread, and a later worker
continues it once the socket is ready.  The sweep thread applies
the four limits above to a parked connection once a second, and the
server applies them again when the connection becomes ready.  A
streaming handler reads its body on its own thread, and there the
limits bound that wait directly.

min_transfer_rate_bps is the floor of the average rate of a body read
and of a response write, measured from the start of each, and judged
once min_transfer_rate_grace_us of it has passed.  It closes a client
that stays just inside every gap limit and still moves almost nothing:
a body below the floor gets 408 and a close, a response below it is cut
off and closed, and chttpsvr_req_read() of a streaming handler fails
with ccol_timed_out.  240 bytes a second after 5 s is the minimum data
rate of Kestrel and of IIS.  Time that the server itself holds a
request back, while it waits for body memory or in the streaming
queue, never counts against the client.

max_partial_body_memory caps the memory that the bodies of buffered
requests hold together on this server, from the first byte that the
server reads until the handler returns.  A reservation covers the
memory that the buffer of a body really holds, and it lasts as long as
that buffer.  The server frees a body, and gives its reservation back,
as soon as its handler returns, before the response goes out, so a
response that waits for a slow reader holds no body; a request that is
refused, cut off or closed frees both at once.  A body with a
Content-Length reserves its whole length before the server reads it,
and its buffer takes exactly that length.  A chunked body reserves its
buffer as the buffer grows, and a growth reserves the old buffer beside
the new one until the copy ends.  A body that does not fit waits, with
no thread, and nothing reads it and no "100 Continue" invites it until
memory frees.  Waiters are admitted in the order in which they began to
wait.  When every body that holds memory is itself waiting for more,
the oldest waiter goes ahead past the limit, one request at a time, so
the limit is exceeded by less than twice max_body_size and the server
never deadlocks.  A body whose handler runs is not waiting: a request
that a handler waits for, and whose body does not fit beside the bodies
that running handlers hold, waits until its deadline.  A waiter that
reaches body_memory_wait_timeout_us gets 503 with "Retry-After: 5" and
a close.

streaming_thread_count sizes the pool that runs streaming handlers,
which the server creates at the first streaming request.  Slow
streaming clients can occupy at most that pool, and never the threads
of buffered routes.  A streaming request that finds every streaming
thread busy waits, with no thread, in a queue of
streaming_queue_capacity entries.  A full queue answers 503 with
"Retry-After: 5" at once, and a request that waits longer than
streaming_queue_timeout_us gets the same answer.

Use CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED for worker_queue_capacity or
streaming_queue_capacity to create a queue with no limit on its
capacity.

Change one field at a time after you apply the macro:

  chttpsvr_config_t cfg = CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT;
  cfg.port = 9090;
  cfg.worker_thread_count = 8;
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_start (3)
